  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The susceptibility model relies on remote sensing data from a single point in time (January 2024), which may not capture seasonal variations in vegetation and land cover that influence runoff dynamics.",
      "what": "This study mapped flood susceptibility in the Ilorin Metropolis, Nigeria, by integrating nine environmental and infrastructural factors derived from January 2024 ASTER GDEM and Landsat 8 imagery using an Analytical Hierarchy Process (AHP). The resulting model classified 42.8% of the metropolitan area as highly or very highly susceptible to flooding, with the greatest risk concentrated along the Asa River corridor and low-lying urban zones. Validation against 200 reference points demonstrated an overall classification accuracy of 96.5%, a sensitivity of 1.00, and a Cohen's kappa of 0.919.",
      "why": "It provides a highly accurate, validated spatial baseline of flood risk for a rapidly growing Nigerian metropolis, offering local planners precise geographic targets for flood mitigation and infrastructure development where empirical data was previously scarce."
    }
  },
